A DI container's internals as a second, different-layer example: Spring's `ApplicationContext` uses a `BeanFactory` internally to construct each registered bean.

```java
@Bean
public PaymentService paymentService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
    return new PaymentService(gateway);
}
```

Under the hood, the container's `BeanFactory` looks at this method, resolves `PaymentGateway` as a dependency, constructs it first, then calls this method to construct `PaymentService` — a Factory doing dependency resolution and construction, hidden behind annotations you never see the factory call itself.
